/A <<
/BBox [60.4913 325.531 540.491 741.165]
/O /Layout
>>
/Alt (The image presents multiple panels with data comparing Parkinson's disease \(PD\), Lewy body dementia \(LID\), and other conditions using neural signal analysis and classification accuracy. Panel A shows a confusion matrix for a classifier distinguishing PD and LID. The matrix indicates high accuracy with 87.16% of PD cases correctly predicted as PD, 87.55% of LID cases correctly predicted as LID, and smaller percentages of misclassification between the two and other categories. Panel B displays bar graphs of mean classification accuracy percentages for different neural signal features and machine learning models. For PD, the high-low machine learning model applied to H-&nobreak;Lo data achieves about 95% accuracy, higher than STN beta median at about 50%. For LID, the high-low machine learning model applied to H-&nobreak;Lo data achieves about 90% accuracy, higher than M1 gamma median at about 40%. Statistical significance is indicated between compared groups. Panel C is a circular plot showing frequency distributions of neural signals across brain regions labeled GPi, DS, M1, PPN, SN, STN, GPe, and GP, with frequency ranges from 0.5 to 130 Hertz. The plot shows varying frequency power levels across regions. Panel D contains three circular plots comparing frequency distributions between PD vs LID, PD vs normal \(Nor\), and LID vs normal. Each plot shows differences in frequency power across the same brain regions and frequency bands as panel C. Panel E shows a line graph of the proportion of predicted classes \(OOD, PD, LID\) over days before and after model training. PD predictions increase sharply after day 12, while OOD and LID predictions decrease or remain low. Panel F shows a line graph of predicted class proportions over time points before and after L-&nobreak;DOPA administration. OOD predictions decrease after administration, PD predictions increase, and LID predictions remain low. Panel G shows a scatter plot with a positive correlation \(r = 0.892, p &#x0003C; 0.001\) between LID scores and the proportion of predicted LID classes, indicating that higher LID scores correspond to higher predicted LID classification. Overall, the figure presents classification performance and neural signal frequency characteristics distinguishing PD, LID, and other conditions, with temporal changes related to treatment and strong correlation between predicted and clinical LID scores.)
